Relevant legislation is the applicable law of the country where the commodity was grown, harvested, obtained, or raised—not generally the law of a country where later manufacturing occurs [EC FAQ Q3.3 (part 1)]. It comprises laws concerning the legal status of the production area, including:

- land-use rights;
- environmental protection;
- forest rules, including forest management and biodiversity conservation directly related to wood harvesting;
- third-party rights;
- labour rights;
- internationally protected human rights;
- free, prior and informed consent (FPIC); and
- tax, anti-corruption, trade, and customs rules [EUDR Art. 2(40)].

These may include national or regional laws, relevant secondary legislation, and international law applicable through domestic law [EC Guidance §6(b) (part 1)]. The Regulation does not identify particular legal acts; their relevance depends on whether they specifically affect or influence the legal status of the production area [EC FAQ Q3.3 (part 1)][EC Guidance §6(a) (part 1)].

