“Primary forest” means a forest that is:

- naturally regenerated;
- composed of native tree species;
- without clearly visible indications of human activities; and
- subject to ecological processes that are not significantly disturbed [EUDR Art. 2(8)].

The definition does not further specify how to assess “clearly visible indications,” “significantly disturbed,” or “native tree species.” The term is relevant to the Regulation’s forest-degradation rules, which cover conversion of primary forests into plantation forests, other wooded land, or planted forests [EUDR Art. 2(7)]. Commission guidance refers to establishing whether the forest type before and including 31 December 2020 was primary forest or naturally regenerating forest [EC FAQ Q4.6 (part 1)].

